<p><br />Older installations may display SkyKick Cloud Manager names. Match on Application ID rather than display name, and record the local Object ID as well. Do not assume a similarly named application has the same identity or status.</p>
<p><strong>1. Verify local status and preserve evidence</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Sign in to https://entra.microsoft.com/, select the correct directory, and record its tenant ID.</li>
<li>Open Entra ID &rarr; Enterprise applications &rarr; All applications. Find each listed Application ID. For an affected application that remains enabled, open Properties, set Enabled for users to sign-in? to No, and save.</li>
<li>Verify the saved state. Record the application and local Object IDs, administrator, and UTC time. Keep affected connections disabled. Do not delete the application as the first response; preserve its configuration and evidence for investigation.</li>
<li>Open Monitoring &amp; health &rarr; Audit logs and download the available records as JSON. Export the available interactive-user, non-interactive-user, service-principal, and managed-identity sign-in records separately. Use smaller date ranges or approved collection tools for large exports.</li>
<li>In Microsoft Purview &rarr; Audit, collect available Microsoft 365 activity covering the investigation period. Preserve relevant SIEM, storage, and previously configured Graph activity records as well.</li>
</ul>
<p>Preserve evidence promptly, but do not delay urgent containment while waiting for exports. Entra normally retains audit and sign-in records for seven days on Free and 30 days on P1/P2; access and features vary. Exported/SIEM retention may be longer. Note missing records and actual coverage. A license upgrade does not recover expired history.</p>
<p><strong>2. Review sign-ins across identities</strong></p>
<p>Review from <strong>October 13, 2026, through the current time</strong>. For broader scoping, extend sign-in searches back to October 1 where records are available, and investigate earlier activity if identified. Record the timezone used.</p>
<ul>
<li>In each available sign-in category, filter IP address to 144.172.64.128. Inspect successful and failed events separately and note any other user or application involved.</li>
<li>In Service principal sign-ins, filter each listed Application ID. Review source addresses, results, timestamps, resources, and available correlation IDs. Expand grouped records to inspect individual events.</li>
<li>Review unfamiliar addresses against the application owner&rsquo;s expected activity. Do not restrict the search to the published IP or assume Microsoft/private addresses establish legitimate use.</li>
</ul>
<table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; border-spacing: 0px; background-color: #ffffff; border: 1px solid #000000;" border="1">
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col" style="width: 29.396%; border-width: 1px; padding: 12px; border-color: #000000;"><strong>Indicator or context</strong></th>
<th scope="col" style="width: 70.618%; border-width: 1px; padding: 12px; border-color: #000000;"><strong>How to interpret it</strong></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="width: 29.396%; border-width: 1px; padding: 12px; border-color: #000000;">144.172.64.128</td>
<td style="width: 70.618%; border-width: 1px; padding: 12px; border-color: #000000;">Published suspicious address. A matching event requires investigation; its result and subsequent activity determine the finding.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="width: 29.396%; border-width: 1px; padding: 12px; border-color: #000000;">Python-urllib/3.12; python-requests; curl</td>
<td style="width: 70.618%; border-width: 1px; padding: 12px; border-color: #000000;">Scripting clients may help identify unusual operations, but are also used by legitimate administrators.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="width: 29.396%; border-width: 1px; padding: 12px; border-color: #000000;">kiota-dotnet; Azure private IPv6</td>
<td style="width: 70.618%; border-width: 1px; padding: 12px; border-color: #000000;">Associated with expected vendor activity. Neither alone establishes that an event was authorized.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><br />Treat attempts, successful authentication, configuration changes, and data access as distinct findings. Common tooling or an address match alone does not prove compromise or exfiltration.</p>
<p><strong>3. Review directory audit events</strong></p>
<p>Open Monitoring &amp; health &rarr; Audit logs. Use October 13 through now and inspect the following activities; portal labels can vary slightly.</p>
<table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; border-spacing: 0px; background-color: #ffffff; border: 1px solid #000000;" border="1">
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col" style="width: 31.7992%; border-width: 1px; padding: 12px; border-color: #000000;"><strong>Category</strong></th>
<th scope="col" style="width: 68.2118%; border-width: 1px; padding: 12px; border-color: #000000;"><strong>Activity examples</strong></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="width: 31.7992%; border-width: 1px; padding: 12px; border-color: #000000;">ApplicationManagement</td>
<td style="width: 68.2118%; border-width: 1px; padding: 12px; border-color: #000000;">Update application &ndash; Certificates and secrets management; Add service principal credentials; Add application; Add service principal; Add owner to application; Add owner to service principal; Add app role assignment to service principal; Add delegated permission grant; Consent to application</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="width: 31.7992%; border-width: 1px; padding: 12px; border-color: #000000;">RoleManagement</td>
<td style="width: 68.2118%; border-width: 1px; padding: 12px; border-color: #000000;">Add member to role; relevant user/group eligible-role assignment changes.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="width: 31.7992%; border-width: 1px; padding: 12px; border-color: #000000;">Policy</td>
<td style="width: 68.2118%; border-width: 1px; padding: 12px; border-color: #000000;">Conditional Access policy additions, changes or deletions; named-location changes.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="width: 31.7992%; border-width: 1px; padding: 12px; border-color: #000000;">UserManagement</td>
<td style="width: 68.2118%; border-width: 1px; padding: 12px; border-color: #000000;">New users, password resets, and authentication-method registration changes.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><br />Open relevant events and record the initiator, target, outcome, correlation ID and modified properties. Review the user agent where recorded; not every event contains it. Search listed applications as both initiators and targets. Follow any changes they made to other identities. Confirm authorization against owner and change records before classifying an unfamiliar event as malicious.</p>
<p><strong>4. Check credentials and federation trusts</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>For locally registered applications, open App registrations &rarr; All applications, confirm the Application ID, and inspect Certificates &amp; secrets, including client secrets, certificates and federated credentials.</li>
<li>Record credential identifiers, thumbprints, validity periods and federation issuer/subject/audience. Compare them with approved configuration and audit events; validity dates alone do not establish when a credential was added.</li>
<li>Review both the application registration and associated service principal. Vendor registrations may not appear in the customer&rsquo;s App registrations. An authorized responder can inspect service-principal credential metadata through Microsoft Graph.</li>
<li>Preserve the evidence, then coordinate removal of unauthorized entries with the owner and incident response provider. Review all management and security integrations that could retain access, not just the seven listed applications.</li>
</ul>
<p>For service-principal secrets, Microsoft Graph provides the removePassword operation using the local Object ID and credential keyId; application-owned secrets use the application equivalent. Certificates and federation trusts have their own operations. Use an appropriately authorized responder and verify removal. Do not assume ConnectWise has access to make these changes inside your tenant.</p>
<p><strong>5. Check roles applications owners and consent</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Open Roles and administrators and review active application assignments, prioritizing Global Administrator, Privileged Role Administrator, Privileged Authentication Administrator, Application Administrator, Cloud Application Administrator, Exchange Administrator and SharePoint Administrator.</li>
<li>Match local Object IDs and correlate changes with audit records. Separately inspect privileged user/group changes and applicable eligible assignments; do not assume application identities use the same activation process as people.</li>
<li>Inspect recently created application registrations and enterprise applications, their Owners, and the enterprise application Permissions grants. Look for unauthorized owners, admin/user consent and elevated API permissions.</li>
<li>Preserve findings and have the authorized responder remove confirmed unauthorized assignments, owners or grants. For a malicious newly created application, disable it, retain evidence, and decide on deletion after investigation.</li>
</ul>
<p>Review each access mechanism separately. If an MSP management identity has permissions across customers, assess which other tenants need investigation. A replacement legitimate credential does not remove an attacker-added credential, role, or consent grant.</p>
<p><strong>6. Check policy changes</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Open Protection &rarr; Conditional Access &rarr; Policies and Named locations. Review recent changes to policy state, assignments, exclusions and trusted locations against approved records.</li>
<li>Escalate unexplained changes and have the policy administrator correct confirmed unauthorized settings. Assess operational effects before altering legitimate controls.</li>
</ul>
<p>This is a review for additional persistence or weakened controls. User policies do not automatically protect application-only authentication. Conditional Access for workload identities covers supported single-tenant service principals registered in the tenant, not third-party SaaS/multitenant apps. It is not a substitute for disabling affected connections.</p>
<p><strong>7. Check Exchange and resource activity</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>In Purview Audit, search the investigation period for Exchange activities such as New-InboxRule, Set-InboxRule, Set-Mailbox, Add-MailboxPermission, Add-RecipientPermission, New-TransportRule and Set-TransportRule. Use corresponding friendly names where the search interface requires them.</li>
<li>Investigate unexpected external forwarding, rules that hide or delete messages, new Full Access or Send As rights, and transport-rule changes. Validate against authorized changes.</li>
<li>Use Exchange admin center &rarr; Mail flow &rarr; Rules for transport rules and Recipients &rarr; Mailboxes for forwarding/delegation settings. Inspect inbox rules separately with authorized Exchange Online PowerShell where needed, including Get-InboxRule -Mailbox &lt;mailbox&gt; -IncludeHidden for hidden rules.</li>
<li>Where available and licensed, correlate MailItemsAccessed, other Microsoft 365 audit records, security alerts and previously collected Graph activity with suspicious events. Include relevant SharePoint, OneDrive and Teams activity.</li>
</ul>
<p>Preserve evidence and remediate confirmed unauthorized changes through your response provider. Application authentication alone does not establish data copying. Normal backup reads and limited logging may make attribution difficult. Enabling logging now does not recover events that were never recorded; document that uncertainty.</p>
<p><strong>8. Review remaining connections and record the outcome</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Search Enterprise applications for other ConnectWise and SkyKick identities. Record their IDs, owners, permissions and purpose. Assess their legitimacy and risk with your security provider and ConnectWise; do not infer compromise from a name alone.</li>
<li>Keep affected or suspicious connections disabled while the review continues. Preserve evidence and assess business impact before removing unrelated legitimate integrations.</li>
<li>For each tenant, record log coverage, findings, containment times, credential/role/consent/policy/mailbox remediation, unresolved questions, and your response owner.</li>
<li>Check the last successful backup recovery point and assess the protection gap while services are unavailable. Follow formal restoration guidance before reconnecting; after authorized restoration, verify workload connections and successful backup jobs.</li>
</ul>
<p>Escalate unauthorized credentials, privileged assignments, suspicious successful activity or evidence of data access to your incident response provider immediately. Send relevant findings to <a href="mailto:SecurityPartnerSupport@ConnectWise.com">SecurityPartnerSupport@ConnectWise.com</a>, including tenant and application identifiers, UTC timestamps, event/correlation IDs and a description. Do not include passwords, private keys, tokens PII or other secrets.</p>
<p>A completed checklist records the work performed and evidence available. It does not guarantee that a tenant was unaffected. Please use the Trust Center for current status and guidance. Contact Partner Care for service assistance and report suspicious activity to <a href="mailto:SecurityPartnerSupport@ConnectWise.com">SecurityPartnerSupport@ConnectWise.com</a>.</p>
