<p><span style="color: #24326a;"><strong></strong></span></p>
<p><br /><br /></p>
<p id="cloud-backup-update-2026-10-17-1700-utc"><span style="color: #24326a;"><strong>Update: Saturday, October 17, 2026 at 17:00 UTC</strong></span></p>
<p><strong>Cloud Backup investigation and service update&nbsp;</strong></p>
<p>We have taken protective action following unauthorized access to application identities associated with ConnectWise Cloud Backup, formerly known as SkyKick. Our investigation remains ongoing. Since implementing the mitigations described below, we have not observed successful threat activity in the environments we monitor.&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong>Actions taken&nbsp;</strong></p>
<p>Out of an abundance of caution, we suspended Cloud Backup service by revoking certificates used for affected application connections across all users. This removed ConnectWise&rsquo;s access through those connections. The shared credentials previously used for Cloud Backup are no longer valid and cannot be used.&nbsp;</p>
<p>In addition to mobilizing our internal security teams and Security Operations Center (SOC), we have engaged a reputable third-party incident response firm to support our investigation and containment efforts. Together, these teams are actively monitoring our backup infrastructure and broader environment, with the investigation extending across all partner-facing services. At this time, we have no indication that any other ConnectWise product, service, or offering is impacted.&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong>Backup data and customer environments&nbsp;</strong></p>
<p>Cloud Backup archives remain encrypted and isolated. We have no evidence that these encrypted backup archives have been accessed. Existing archives are isolated and preserved, although access and restoration remain unavailable during the service interruption.&nbsp;</p>
<p>These findings relate to our Cloud Backup environment. By design, the configuration of Cloud Backup does not provide us visibility or access to downstream partner and customer environments. As a result, we cannot determine through our investigation alone whether a particular customer&rsquo;s Microsoft 365 environment experienced unauthorized activity. Partners should continue reviewing their environments with their security teams or managed security providers.&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong>Investigation and restoration&nbsp;</strong></p>
<p>We are in the second phase of our investigation and response, validating critical findings and mitigation paths. This work will determine the steps required to restore service. We will communicate when we exit this phase and share restoration timing and any required partner actions as soon as possible.&nbsp;</p>
<p>We appreciate the cybersecurity community&rsquo;s support and contributions to the investigation. We recognize the urgency of restoring protection for our partners and their customers.&nbsp;</p>
<p>We will publish another update later today, October 17, 2026, through the ConnectWise Trust Center, including if there is no material change. Please use the Trust Center for current status and guidance. Contact Partner Care for service assistance and report suspicious activity to <a href="mailto:SecurityPartnerSupport@ConnectWise.com">SecurityPartnerSupport@ConnectWise.com</a>.&nbsp;</p>
<p>We understand the disruption this incident is causing our partners and their customers, particularly while backup and restoration services remain unavailable. Our teams are working urgently to restore service.</p>
<p><br /><br /></p>
<p id="cloud-backup-update-2026-10-17-0501-utc"><span style="color: #24326a;"><strong>Update: Saturday, October 17, 2026 at 05:01 UTC</strong></span><br /><br />We recently identified unauthorized access to application identities within the ConnectWise Cloud Backup environment.</p>
<p><strong>As a protective measure for our Cloud Backup partners and their clients, we are shutting off Cloud Backup application access. This will interrupt backup services for customers until further notice.</strong></p>
<p>We have no indicators to date that Cloud Backup partner backup data has been compromised. <strong>The existing cloud backup archive is not impacted by this service interruption and will be accessible once services are restored.</strong> Our investigation and remediation efforts remain ongoing.</p>
<p><strong>How to Check for Indicators of Compromise (IOCs)</strong></p>
<p>If you would like to review your environment for potential indicators of compromise associated with this activity, follow the steps below:</p>
<ul>
<li>In the Microsoft Entra Admin Center, navigate to Monitoring &rarr; Sign-in logs, select the Service principal sign-ins tab, and filter by Application ID 506e08f9-6e9a-4da4-942b-a099a03644ae.
<ul>
<li>This is where the application's authentication activity is recorded.</li>
<li>The default User sign-ins tab will not display the required information.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>In the IP address column, review authentication activity originating from IP addresses outside expected Microsoft/Azure ranges. The application's normal egress has been identified as Azure private IPv6.
<ul>
<li>Identified suspicious IP address: 144.172.64.128</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p>The presence of these indicators warrants further investigation but does not, by itself, confirm that your environment has been compromised.</p>
<p>Please check back with our Trust Center for updates and published guidance on monitoring your environment. Coordinate with your security team or managed security provider to review unusual application sign-ins and permission changes. <strong>Report suspicious activity to <a href="mailto:SecurityPartnerSupport@ConnectWise.com">SecurityPartnerSupport@ConnectWise.com</a>.</strong></p>
<p>We recognize the impact on our partners' operations and will provide further updates, including service status through the Trust Center and Partner Care.</p>
<p>Thank you,<br />ConnectWise</p>
<p id="cloud-backup-update-2026-10-16-1747-utc"><br /><br /><span style="color: #24326a;"><strong>Update: Friday, October 16, 2026 at 17:47 UTC</strong></span></p>
<p>ConnectWise is investigating recent attempts of unauthorized access associated with the ConnectWise Cloud Backup environment. Upon detecting this threat, we promptly initiated our incident response procedures. Containment measures are in place, and our investigation continues. At this time, there is no indication that partner backup data has been accessed.</p>
<p>Our engineering teams are continuing to explore the nature and scope of the issue. Our investigation is ongoing, and we will provide additional information as appropriate. We appreciate your patience while we work through this process.</p>
<h6>Indicators of Compromise (IOCs)</h6>
<p>The following indicators have been associated with the reported suspicious authentication activity.</p>
<p><strong>Source IP Address</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><code>144.172.64.128</code></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Observed User Agents</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><code>Python-urllib/3.12</code></li>
<li><code>python-requests/2.31</code></li>
</ul>
<p>These indicators are provided in transparency to assist with security monitoring and investigation. Their presence alone does not establish unauthorized activity or confirm compromise.<br /><br /></p>
<h4 id="faqs" style="text-align: center; scroll-margin-top: 120px;"><span style="color: #24326a;">FAQs</span></h4>
<p style="margin-bottom: 24px; text-align: left;"><strong>1. Who was impacted?</strong><br />The service interruption affects partners and customers using Cloud Backup, formerly known as SkyKick. Because SaaS Security and Cloud Management are integrated with Cloud Backup, those services have also been suspended out of an abundance of caution while our investigation and remediation continue. A service suspension does not, by itself, mean that a partner or customer environment was compromised.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 24px; text-align: left;"><strong>2. Which ConnectWise products and services are affected?</strong><br />Cloud Backup is currently suspended in response to observed indicators of unauthorized activity. Because SaaS Security and Cloud Management are integrated with Cloud Backup, we have also suspended those services out of an abundance of caution while investigation and remediation continue.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 24px; text-align: left;"><strong>3. What impact does this have on other ConnectWise products?</strong><br />The current service interruption applies to Cloud Backup, SaaS Security and Cloud Management. We will communicate any changes to the scope of affected services as the investigation progresses.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 24px; text-align: left;"><strong>4. When did you find out there was a problem?</strong><br />Upon discovery on October 14, 2026, ConnectWise initiated internal and external incident investigation and response actions.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 24px; text-align: left;"><strong>5. Who was the threat actor?<br /></strong>Based on the evidence available to ConnectWise, including threat intelligence shared by Microsoft, it appears that a China state-aligned group was responsible for the attack on ConnectWise Cloud Backup. The group used an openly available agentic AI framework to automate and speed up its operation.</p>
<p>Microsoft's <a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/10/21/storm-0940-uses-artex-agentic-ai-connectwise-supply-chain-compromise/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">research</a> describes a campaign that reaches well beyond any one company. This group has been active since 2021, and since at least September it has used the same AI tooling against organizations in aerospace and defense, energy, financial services, government, legal, manufacturing, semiconductors and technology. Microsoft also reports that threat actors in multiple regions are now using the same framework, and that in several cases attackers reached privileged cloud access in under three hours.</p>
<p>Nation-state actors are increasingly targeting the software supply chain, because one trusted connection can open the door to many organizations. AI now lets them test those connections at what Microsoft calls 'machine speed'. MSPs are in the middle of that chain. They are targets in their own right, and they are also a path into every client they serve. Every vendor and every MSP must defend itself with both of those risks in mind, and we intend to share what we are learning from this incident so our partners can do that.</p>
<p>Our first responsibility is to our partners. We contained the affected application connections and are restoring services in controlled phases. Those actions do not remove unauthorized access that may already have been established within individual customer tenants, which require separate review. We are also working with independent specialists so partners have the evidence they need to review their own tenants. Partners should keep affected connections disabled until we issue formal instructions, and should follow the current guidance on the <a href="/company/trust/advisories#cloudbackup">ConnectWise Trust Center.</a></p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 24px; text-align: left;"><strong>6. Was there unauthorized access to my data?</strong><br />We have no indication to date that partner backup data has been compromised. We continue investigating potential unauthorized access and will communicate material findings affecting partners or their customers, if any.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 24px; text-align: left;"><strong>7. Is my environment compromised?</strong><br />We have not identified confirmed downstream customer compromise. However, the investigation remains ongoing, and the absence of confirmed compromise is not assurance that no unauthorized activity occurred.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 24px; text-align: left;"><strong>8. Are they on my network?</strong><br />We have not identified any instance of the attacker accessing your internal network or endpoints. Access through a cloud application identity does not, by itself, establish internal network access. Potential downstream activity remains under investigation.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 24px; text-align: left;"><strong>9. Was there a product vulnerability?</strong><br />The investigation identified compromised application identities. Whether a product vulnerability contributed has not been established, and the original credential-exposure mechanism remains under investigation.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 24px; text-align: left;"><strong>10. Why did you disable my service?</strong><br />Cloud Backup was suspended in response to observed indicators of unauthorized activity while we investigate and remediate the incident. Because SaaS Security and Cloud Management are integrated with Cloud Backup, we also suspended those services out of an abundance of caution. These measures are intended to reduce risk while we work to restore service safely.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 24px; text-align: left;"><strong>11. What steps have you taken to contain the threat?</strong><br />We have rotated credentials or disabled the compromised application identities identified to date and are reviewing and reducing application permissions. We continue authentication monitoring and forensic analysis, and we are disabling affected Cloud Backup application access as an additional protective measure.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 24px; text-align: left;"><strong>12. Did I lose my backup?</strong><br />Backup and restoration services are currently unavailable while Cloud Backup remains suspended. Our investigation and remediation are ongoing, and we will provide additional information about service restoration as it is confirmed.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 24px; text-align: left;"><strong>13. Can I restore data during the interruption?</strong><br />Backup and restoration services remain unavailable while Cloud Backup is suspended. We recognize the impact this has on partners and their customers and are working with third-party specialists to restore service safely and as quickly as possible.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 24px; text-align: left;"><strong>14. What should I look for in my environment?</strong><br />Follow the steps in the technical advisory to review your Microsoft 365 environment and each customer environment you manage. The guidance covers affected application identities, authentication and audit activity, credentials, permissions, role assignments, mailbox changes and other potential persistence.<br /><br />Partners should also include the following IP addresses when reviewing available Microsoft 365 authentication and audit logs:<br /><br /><strong>144.172.64[.]99</strong><br /><strong>144.172.64[.]60</strong><br /><strong>144.172.64[.]128</strong><br /><br />An observed connection to one of these IP addresses should be investigated in context and does not, by itself, establish compromise.</p>
<p><strong>15. I found application ID c921256f-04a7-44e8-9b2a-69c33929ba19 in my tenant. Should I be concerned?<br /></strong>This is an application ID associated with a ConnectWise application. &nbsp;While no direct threat was observed, we do recommend disabling the application in your environment until we restore all services and inform you that it is safe to re-enroll for access to the application. As part of our initial containment measures, ConnectWise disabled the application in our environment.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 24px; text-align: left;"><strong>16. What steps should I take to contain the threat?</strong><br />Follow the technical advisory for detailed steps to review your MSP Microsoft 365 environment and each customer environment you manage.<br /><br />If you provide these services through a reseller, distributor or marketplace arrangement, ensure the guidance reaches the administrator responsible for each customer tenant. If you do not administer those environments directly, coordinate with the customer&rsquo;s IT administrator or security provider to confirm the guidance has been received and the review is being addressed. Report suspicious activity to SecurityPartnerSupport@ConnectWise.com.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 24px; text-align: left;"><strong>17. What other steps should I take next?</strong><br />Monitor the ConnectWise Trust Center and direct communications. Review relevant activity with your security provider, assess continuity needs while backups are interrupted, and report suspicious findings.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 24px; text-align: left;"><strong>18. When will service be restored?<br /></strong>As previously reported, we identified unauthorized access to application credentials associated with Cloud Backup, formerly SkyKick.</p>
<p><strong>Phased restoration is underway, with each step subject to security validation.</strong></p>
<p>We expect restoration to take several days and will share service-specific availability and reconnection instructions as confirmed. <strong>Keep affected connections disabled until formal instructions apply to your service.</strong></p>
<p>Investigation and monitoring continue. Existing backup archives remain encrypted, isolated, and preserved, with no indication of compromise. We have no indication that other ConnectWise products, services, or offerings are impacted by this incident.</p>
<p>Individual Microsoft 365 tenants still require review for unauthorized changes or persistent access. Please follow the <a aria-label="Link technical guide" id="menur324n" href="/contentassets/10777cd3364d440c86ed5270a94a5f24/connectwise-cloud-backup-technical-investigation-and-remediation-guide-1.pdf" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank" class="fui-Link ___1q1shib f2hkw1w f3rmtva f1ewtqcl fyind8e f1k6fduh f1w7gpdv fk6fouc fjoy568 figsok6 f1s184ao f1mk8lai fnbmjn9 f1o700av f13mvf36 f1cmlufx f9n3di6 f1ids18y f1tx3yz7 f1deo86v f1eh06m1 f1iescvh fhgqx19 f1olyrje f1p93eir f1nev41a f1h8hb77 f1lqvz6u f10aw75t fsle3fq f17ae5zn" title="https://www.connectwise.com/contentassets/10777cd3364d440c86ed5270a94a5f24/connectwise-cloud-backup-technical-investigation-and-remediation-guide-1.pdf">technical guide</a> and <a aria-label="Link technical webinars" id="menur324q" href="/webinars/cloud-backup-technical-investigation-and-remediation" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank" class="fui-Link ___1q1shib f2hkw1w f3rmtva f1ewtqcl fyind8e f1k6fduh f1w7gpdv fk6fouc fjoy568 figsok6 f1s184ao f1mk8lai fnbmjn9 f1o700av f13mvf36 f1cmlufx f9n3di6 f1ids18y f1tx3yz7 f1deo86v f1eh06m1 f1iescvh fhgqx19 f1olyrje f1p93eir f1nev41a f1h8hb77 f1lqvz6u f10aw75t fsle3fq f17ae5zn" title="https://www.connectwise.com/webinars/cloud-backup-technical-investigation-and-remediation">technical webinars</a> for your own and managed customer environments.</p>
<p>We recognize the continued disruption and remain focused on restoring service safely.</p>
<p>Beginning October 22, we will publish updates twice daily, with significant developments shared sooner.</p>
<p>Visit the <a aria-label="Link Trust Center" id="menur324t" href="/company/trust/advisories" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank" class="fui-Link ___1q1shib f2hkw1w f3rmtva f1ewtqcl fyind8e f1k6fduh f1w7gpdv fk6fouc fjoy568 figsok6 f1s184ao f1mk8lai fnbmjn9 f1o700av f13mvf36 f1cmlufx f9n3di6 f1ids18y f1tx3yz7 f1deo86v f1eh06m1 f1iescvh fhgqx19 f1olyrje f1p93eir f1nev41a f1h8hb77 f1lqvz6u f10aw75t fsle3fq f17ae5zn" title="https://www.connectwise.com/company/trust/advisories">Trust Center</a> for updates. Contact Partner Care for service assistance and report suspicious activity to <a aria-label="Link SecurityPartnerSupport@ConnectWise.com" id="menur3250" href="mailto:SecurityPartnerSupport@ConnectWise.com" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank" class="fui-Link ___1q1shib f2hkw1w f3rmtva f1ewtqcl fyind8e f1k6fduh f1w7gpdv fk6fouc fjoy568 figsok6 f1s184ao f1mk8lai fnbmjn9 f1o700av f13mvf36 f1cmlufx f9n3di6 f1ids18y f1tx3yz7 f1deo86v f1eh06m1 f1iescvh fhgqx19 f1olyrje f1p93eir f1nev41a f1h8hb77 f1lqvz6u f10aw75t fsle3fq f17ae5zn" title="mailto:securitypartnersupport@connectwise.com">SecurityPartnerSupport@ConnectWise.com</a>.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 24px; text-align: left;"><strong>19. Will I need to reconnect or reauthorize customer tenants?</strong><br />Restoration requirements are still being determined. If reauthorization or other partner action is required, we will provide instructions.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 24px; text-align: left;"><strong>20. How do I turn my service back on?</strong><br />Wait for ConnectWise to confirm that restoration can proceed. Follow the reconnection or reauthorization instructions provided through direct communications and the Trust Center. Once restored, verify that protected workloads are connected and scheduled backups have resumed.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 24px; text-align: left;"><strong>21. How do I report suspicious activity?</strong><br />Email SecurityPartnerSupport@ConnectWise.com. Include affected tenant and application identifiers, timestamps and time zones, and a description of the activity. Do not include passwords or private keys.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 24px; text-align: left;"><strong>22. Where can I find updates and support?</strong><br />Visit the ConnectWise Trust Center for published updates and guidance. Contact Partner Care for service-related assistance.<br /><br /><br /><br /></p>
<p><br /><br /></p>
<h2>September 3, 2026: ScreenConnect&reg; Remote Access: Guest File Transfer Advisory | Updated September 9, 2026</h2>
<p><em><span data-contrast="none">This advisory was originally published on September 3, 2026, to provide interim mitigation guidance before the security update became generally available.</span></em></p>
<p><span data-contrast="none">The remediation is now available in ScreenConnect 26.6.5 or higher. For the latest technical information, affected versions, remediation guidance, and CVE details, please refer to:</span></p>
<ul>
<li><span data-contrast="none">Security Bulletin: <a href="/company/trust/security-bulletins/2026-09-08-screenconnect-bulletin" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2026-09-08 ScreenConnect Bulletin</a></span></li>
<li><span data-contrast="none">CVE-2026-84869</span></li>
</ul>
<p><span data-contrast="none"><span>ConnectWise has identified an issue affecting file transfer behavior in ScreenConnect&reg; Remote Access Support and Access sessions. The issue affects both Cloud and On-Premise deployments.</span></span></p>
<p><b><b><span data-contrast="none"><span data-ccp-parastyle="heading 1">Affected Product</span></span></b><span data-ccp-props="{&quot;134245418&quot;:true,&quot;134245529&quot;:true,&quot;335559738&quot;:300,&quot;335559739&quot;:100}"> <br /></span></b><em><span data-contrast="none">What's in scope for this advisory.</span><span data-ccp-props="{&quot;335559739&quot;:100}">&nbsp;</span></em></p>
